{
  "abstract": "We study extensions of the explicit 38-vertex, 193-edge nine-critical biplanar graph of clique number six. Keep the source induced, add three mutually adjacent hubs with arbitrary source neighborhoods, and add independent leaves, each complete to the hubs and one source six-clique. We prove that every biplanar extension of this form with at least six leaves is nine-colorable. More generally, at most six leaves and at most twenty hub–source incidences suffice whenever the graph is $K_9$-free; seventeen incidences suffice for arbitrarily many leaves without a drawing or clique hypothesis. The main difficulty is that a hypothetical critical support may delete source vertices. Degree accounting, Gallai's low-degree structure and simultaneous palette choices force such a support to lose an entire low pentagon and its private high vertices. Four independently reconstructed and RUP-certified interface formulas then force a high four-clique adjacent to all hubs. A minimal-list argument forces a common low edge, leaving at most two further incidences. For each of the 197 possible noncomplete common six-sets, one positive source coloring preserves a spare for every source six-clique; a hub-color permutation absorbs the remaining incidences. A compact public supplement includes all positive words, proof traces and standalone replays. The result closes this attachment family, including arbitrary deleted critical supports. Fewer-leaf extensions with larger contact budgets, altered leaf interactions and the global Earth–Moon problem remain open.",
